<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Move Around – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Move Around</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Show comprehension of basic commands used in the classroom.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of simple movement instructions during songs (e.g., 'jump' or 'clap').
Can repeat simple rhymes and songs in group activities that involve listening for action words (e.g., jumps when the song says 'jump').
Can respond to oral instructions for basic dance movements (e.g., 'step forward, step back').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary related to "Move Around" and "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Listen and Do" activity using basic movement commands. Students stand in a circle. The teacher says "Clap your hands!" or "Wiggle your fingers!" to get them moving. Introduces flashcards for 'jump', 'step', 'spin'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher says a command clearly and demonstrates the action (e.g., "Jump!"). The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key verbs like 'jump' and 'dance', drawing attention to the /J/ sound in 'jump' and the /d/ sound in 'dance'. Students are encouraged to imitate the actions. The teacher uses exaggerated movements and clear, slow speech.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding through TPR and simple questions.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "When I say 'jump', do you stand still or move up and down?" (Teacher demonstrates both). Students point or nod.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Show me 'step'." (Teacher performs 'step'). Students imitate.</li>
                    </ul>
                    The teacher confirms understanding of 'jump', 'step', 'spin', 'shake', 'turn' by demonstrating each and having students mirror.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An engaging action song (e.g., "The Hokey Pokey" or a simplified "If You're Happy and You Know It") and a sequence of spoken dance commands accompanied by colorful visual flashcards depicting actions (jump, step, spin, shake, turn, one, two, three). Focus on simple directive phrases like 'Move to the front', 'Step to the side', and numbers one to three.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Acting out dance commands; Reciting action rhymes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays a short action song (e.g., "The Hokey Pokey"). Students listen and identify if the song makes them want to 'move' or 'sit still' by showing a thumbs up/down or pointing to 'move' flashcard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Follow the Command:</b> The teacher plays simple spoken commands: "Jump one time!" "Step two times!" "Spin around!" Students listen and perform the action immediately (TPR).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Number Recognition:</b> When the teacher says, "Take one step!" students respond by taking one step and showing one finger. When the teacher says, "Take two steps!" students take two steps and show two fingers, reinforcing listening for numbers (one to ten).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening for commands and action words.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Sequential commands with numbers (e.g., 'First, take one step to the side.' 'Next, take two steps to the side.'); Simple directive phrases (e.g., 'Move to the front.' 'Step to the side.'); Positional commands with prepositions (e.g., 'Stand next to the chair.' 'Jump over the line.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> jump, step, spin, turn, shake, move, dance, one, two, three, four, five, chair, line, circle, fast, slow.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Focus on /t/ in 'two' and /d/ in 'dance'. Phonemic Awareness: 'J' in 'jump'.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Move (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher calls out commands one by one, using flashcards for visual support. "First, jump one time!" "Next, spin around!" "Step two steps!" Students listen and perform the actions. The teacher pauses after each command to ensure comprehension and correct execution. This incorporates sequential commands and numbers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Dance Command Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> Students form a line. The teacher gives a command to the first student (e.g., "Move to the front!"). That student performs the action, then the teacher gives a command to the next student (e.g., "Step to the side!"). This continues down the line, increasing complexity by adding prepositions: "Stand next to the chair!" or "Jump over the line!" Teacher emphasizes listening for 'next to' and 'over'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Action Mirror (Group Interaction):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher says a command (e.g., "Shake your hands slowly!"). Students listen and mirror the action. Then the teacher changes speed: "Shake your hands fast!" This practices listening for adjectives 'fast' and 'slow'. The teacher encourages the use of "Go ahead!" or "After you!" in a simple turn-taking context if a student wants to briefly lead a single action, fostering polite group participation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Follow the Dance Leader" group activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher acts as the main dance leader. The teacher leads the class in a simple "Let's Dance!" routine, giving a sequence of commands. "First, move two steps to the front. One, two! Next, spin around! Then, jump three times! One, two, three!" The teacher plays instrumental music, and students listen carefully to the commands and perform the actions as part of a collaborative dance. The teacher ensures every student has space and opportunity to move. Occasional praise for listening and participation ("Good listening, everyone!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly simulates a dance scenario, requiring students to listen and respond to commands to "move around" in a structured way.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension and execution of dance commands, collaborative interaction in a group dance, and participation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives three new, slightly varied commands (e.g., "Dance fast in a circle!", "Put one foot on the line!", "Shake your whole body slowly!"). Students listen and demonstrate understanding through TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurately follows simple movement commands (e.g., 'jump', 'step', 'spin').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to numerical instructions for movements (e.g., 'one step', 'two jumps').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of positional commands (e.g., 'next to', 'over').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participates actively in group movements, using polite expressions (if prompted, e.g., 'after you').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a thumbs up if they understood and could do all the dance moves, a thumbs sideways if they understood some but not all, or a thumbs down if it was difficult. The teacher asks, "Did you listen for 'jump' or 'step'?" Students point to the corresponding action word flashcard or perform the action.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance move today? (Jump, Step, Spin, or Shake?)" Students can demonstrate or point to flashcard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you listen carefully to my voice when I said 'one' or 'two' steps?" (Students nod Yes/No).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR engagement, visual aids, repetition). The students responded well to the "Follow the Dance Leader" game, showing good listening for action verbs and numbers. The phonemic awareness activity for /J/ in 'jump' was effective.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with 'next to' and 'over'; more physical examples and repetition will be beneficial. Incorporate more varied prepositions and potentially introduce 'big' and 'small' to describe movements in the next listening activity.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a simple illustrated story or instructions that use the same core vocabulary (move, jump, step, dance, chair, desk, numbers) to understand the words in a written context, building upon their listening comprehension from this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>